<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maharana Pratap</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1540">1540</a>–<a href="/years/1597">1597</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Pratap Singh I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia-dynasty">Sisodia dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> Pratap was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> ruler of Mewar from 1572 to 1597. He refused to submit to the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> and fought a Mughal army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-i">Man Singh I</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-haldighati">Battle of Haldighati</a> in 1576. Though defeated, he continued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance from the hills and recovered much of Mewar before his death, becoming a lasting symbol of Rajput independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap.jpg" alt="Maharana Pratap" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Surendra Singh Shaktawat (Semliyaheera) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharana_Pratap_By_Surendra_Singh_Shaktawat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pratap Singh was born in 1540, the year his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii">Udai Singh II</a> took the throne of Mewar. His mother was Jaiwanta Bai, and his younger brothers included Shakti Singh, Vikram Singh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagmal-singh">Jagmal Singh</a>. After Udai Singh's death in 1572 his wife Rani Dheer Bai Bhatiyani wanted her son Jagmal to succeed, but senior courtiers preferred Pratap as the eldest son. He was crowned at Gogunda on the day of Holi as the 54th ruler of Mewar in the line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia Rajputs</a>. Jagmal swore revenge and went to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh</a> in 1567 and 1568 had cost Mewar its fertile eastern belt, but the wooded hills of the Aravalli range were still held by Pratap. Akbar wanted a stable route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> through Mewar and sent envoys, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-i">Man Singh I</a> of Amber, Bhagwant Das and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Todar Mal</a>, asking Pratap to become a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. Pratap consistently refused, either with vague promises or by declining to meet them. Akbar's demands probably included attendance at court, tribute, allegiance or a marriage alliance, none of which he would accept.</p><p>On 18 June 1576 Pratap, with about 3,000 cavalry and 400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a> archers, met Man Singh, who commanded about 10,000 men, at Haldighati near Gogunda. After more than three hours Pratap was wounded and the day was lost, but he escaped to the hills and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> did not capture him or his family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> himself campaigned against him in September 1576, and Gogunda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbhalgarh">Kumbhalgarh</a> came under Mughal control. Shahbaz Khan Kamboh's later invasions weakened Pratap further and forced him to take refuge in the hills, where he fought as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a>.</p><p>Mughal pressure eased after 1579 because of rebellions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. A Mughal expedition under Jagannath Kachhwaha in 1584 was driven back, and in 1585 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> for twelve years. Pratap recovered thirty-six outposts, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, Gogunda and Mandal, and by his death he had regained a large part of Mewar. He could not retake Chittor itself. His court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavand">Chavand</a> sheltered poets and artists, and the Chavand school of art developed there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pratap reportedly died of injuries from a hunting accident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavand">Chavand</a> on 19 January 1597, aged 56, and his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amar-singh-i">Amar Singh I</a> succeeded him. He is a folk hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The historian Satish Chandra wrote that his defiance of the Mughal empire, almost alone and unaided by the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states, was a saga of Rajput valour, and that his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> methods were later elaborated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ambar">Malik Ambar</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maharana Pratap” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Maharana Pratap</b> refused submission and fought a Mughal army under Man Singh I of Amber at the Battle of Haldighati on 18 June 1576; the battle was a Mughal tactical victory, but Pratap escaped and waged a long war of raids from the hills, recovering much…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbhalgarh">Kumbhalgarh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Chavand, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.15,73.58&amp;year=1577" aria-label="Kumbhalgarh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6501 1238 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maharana Pratap"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6951 1508h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6951 1508h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.15,73.58&amp;year=1577">What was here in 1577? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
